00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
emails are arguably still the most  important medium of professional  communication in most corporates the  writing a strong email is not easy it's  really a craft a skill that needs to be  trained and developed and practiced so  welcome to another coffee break here on  my channel from learning i'm a former  mckinsey consultant and on my channel i  want to help you to become successful in  the first years of your career and today  i want to talk about writing effective  and well-structured remates so without  further ado let's jump into it i  prepared an email here as a practical  example that now we can together improve  let me briefly read out the email to you  subject summary of kickoff dear team  thank you for your time last week in the  kickoff looking forward to meet you  again in our first dro fix next tuesday  paul can you please schedule it and also  our monthly still costs i very much  enjoyed our discussion on what  initiatives to focus on for the new  markets project we decided to not  further pursue opportunities in the  nordics or eastern europe we agreed to  focus on spain for a pilot for cavic  product line gold 20 euro million  revenue in six months we also need to  conduct consumer focus groups in germany  to develop new product variations at the  end of the year in addition we want to  reduce our engagement in the french  market we therefore want to close down  our state's offices but make sure the  key accounts can still be served from  our european account managers as the  first step i suggest we structure all  these initiatives into project plan  carla can you please set one up for  first your fix next week please everyone  who better prepare for joe fix meetings  i expect to receive regular updates from  the sales marketing and product team  leads in these meetings let's make sure  we are able to provide meaningful  updates to our board in the monthly  stickers thanks everyone best luis so  while this email is not completely  horrible there are for sure several  things that can be improved  significantly and the first is the  subject line so here it says summary of  kickoff well that's not wrong but of  course it's quite generic as a  professional you might deal with  different projects at the same time it's  always better to be a little bit more  precise more specific that you directly  have the context of such an email in the  inbox so maybe rather write something  like project new markets summary kickoff  just to provide this context let's now  talk about the first paragraph of your  email here you should make sure to  always have two things included first  provide some context what is this about  what is the context of what you're  writing in and then second also try to  summarize what exactly you want so if  you want to schedule a meeting with  someone don't just write this in the  last sentence of the email but write  this in the first paragraph this is also  the idea of top-down communication if  you have no idea what top-down  communication is i made a video about  that in the past as well i will link it  somewhere about here and in this example  probably would make sense just to  provide an outline of what you will do  here so you will summarize what has  happened in the kickoff and what the key  topics are that you will write about  again this provides important context  another person knows what to expect when  looking at the rest of the email so  let's next talk about the structure of  an email the core of every good email is  a good structure notice how here the  email is structured in different  paragraphs but these paragraphs do not  seem to follow any logical organization  principle the next paragraph talks about  initiatives first it mentions things  that the team does not want to focus on  the nordics or eastern europe and then  it talks about an initiative that the  team does want to focus on here's spain  for a pilot the third paragraph once  again talks about initiatives first  about what is supposed to happen in  germany then what is supposed to happen  in france and then it is mixed with  information on to-dos and then the last  paragraph again talks about your fixed  meetings the expectations toward these  meetings and then also some info on the  monthly steer course that are apparently  supposed to take place so let's go over  different things that can be improved  here and first let's think about the  logical groups the logical categories  that you want to talk about so in this  email the three types of information  apparent that the person wants to  communicate first the focus initiatives  that are supposed to happen second the  meeting schedule what types of meetings  are supposed to take place and then  third the next steps what are the two  do's that the team members have and when  coming up with these logical categories  always think of the mesi principle that  is of course also applicable here as  well when coming up with these groups  and if you have no idea what the mise  principle is it's a structure principle  that is very commonly used in consulting  in professional services i made a video  about that in the past i will link it  somewhere but here check it out a little  more next try to use bullet point format  whenever possible avoid writing long  sentences like you would write  paragraphs in a book you need far more  words to say the very same things take  out words by using bullet point format  and that again makes it far easier to  process for the person reading this  another little technique that you can  use just to increase the clarity and  provide more structure of your email is  to go beyond just writing bullets but  letting the bullets always start with  one two three words that serve as a  little sub header for the bullets  because now everyone when they read  these bullets they don't even need to  read it word by word but just by reading  one or two words they have an idea what  what this is about can decide for  themselves so there are some errors that  they don't even want to read because  it's not so relevant for them but others  that they want to read more about next  let's talk about distributing to do's  distributing the next steps to your team  and an error that i often see and i  think it's often quite dangerous is if  you want that someone in the team does  certain things you just in the middle of  the email somewhere mention their name  right and of course if these people very  carefully read the whole email they  might spot their name and they might  spot here the information that this is  what they need to do but in general i  think it's a good practice that whenever  you want to address someone in an email  and that person is supposed to do  something you bold that name right so in  the middle of the email you make that  name bold because then of course the  person will quite directly see that this  is where you write about the person and  can then also read on the associated to  do's tasks things that you want the  person to do let's talk about two  additional tips that are maybe not  directly related to this example email  but the first thing that bothers me  quite a lot is this email ping pong that  often happens if you want to schedule a  meeting with someone because what often  happens is that one person proposes  dates then you need to check in your  calendar propose other dates and then  goes back and forth and so to avoid  making this process even more  complicated make it a good habit of  yours that whenever you ask for a  meeting always start with some first  suggestions that you directly share and  of course now there are many more modern  mechanisms with meeting links  schedulings that can speed up this  process even more and the second little  suggestion is to push for action in  these emails because often you have the  bit unpleasant situation that maybe you  ask a question or waiting for feedback  and the people aren't replying you don't  know do they still have a point to share  or not and so on and a little technique  that i found often quite effective is to  always then mix your emails with little  deadlines right so maybe write something  in the end so if you have any feedback  on that or any other point you want to  add please let us know until tomorrow or  2pm and if you don't hear anything from  them until tomorrow 2 pm then you let  them know that you assume that this  email is all right now all that being  said we all know that the number of  emails that is written in many  professional jobs is far too high the  number of emails we receive can really  be overwhelming so let me share three  tips on what some things are that you  should avoid when not to write an email  and i think the first is whenever you  just want to share one or two lines a  casual thought nothing that needs to be  forwarded needs to be acted on rather  void emails right many companies have  solutions like microsoft teams slack  google chat and similar solutions in  place rather use them and rather don't  write an email when it's not absolutely  necessary second to be careful when you  want to use emails to escalate things we  all know this feeling something bothers  you a little bit angry so let's write an  email put lots of people on cc and  complain so of course these are often  also political games and i don't want to  say that this is always the wrong thing  to do but just one tip of advice that  actually i picked up from a high school  teacher back in the day is that that  person told me you know when you are  angry when you really just want to say  something to someone when you want to  write such a message  sleep over it for one night just take a  break sleep over it for one night if the  next day you still feel like that this  is the right thing to do then go for it  but often the next day you see things  far different you feel different about  it and then you might even regret having  written such an email the last day and  then third a tip i picked up from a  partner i used to work with that person  once told me  good news in writing  bad news in person right so if you have  a good news to share something great  that maybe you did or your team achieved  write an email about that why well  because you want to make it permanent  because you want to give the recipient  the opportunity potentially to forward  the good news to other people share the  good message and then this of course  will be something that will often be  positive for you on the other hand avoid  putting bad news in writing maybe you  did a mistake you need to come clean on  it for these things don't write an email  whenever possible pick up the phone have  a face-to-face conversation with the  person you want to talk to and here for  the very same reasons you don't want to  make it too easy for that person just to  forward the email to other people making  it very salient what you did what went  wrong you don't want the person half a  year later in their email inbox search  for your name and then just by chance  finding this email again reminding them  of the things that maybe went badly the  things that you did wrong in the past so  of course this might be a little bit  oversimplified for some situations but  in general the rule of thumb is good  news in writing bad news in person so do  you have any other tips on how to write  effective emails just leave a comment in  the comment section big big thanks to  all the members of firm learning for  making a contribution much appreciated  if you took any value out of this video  at all please destroy the like button  for the youtube algorithm and also  subscribe and turn on the notification  bell my name is heinrich you release  videos every single saturday see you  here again on this channel next week  until then all the best to you and bye

